WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.207
Now build a grouped query for diagnoses with more than one admission.

00:00:04.207 --> 00:00:09.272
Before touching the editor, state the grain: one row per diagnosis.

00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:11.927
Then choose the measure: admission count.

00:00:11.927 --> 00:00:18.187
Because the condition is based on the completed count, decide whether it belongs in WHERE or HAVING.

00:00:18.187 --> 00:00:23.457
Use the starter code in the browser, complete the missing pieces, and run it.

00:00:23.457 --> 00:00:27.452
Check that only diagnoses with more than one admission remain.

00:00:27.452 --> 00:00:34.437
If you are practicing in pgAdmin, you can copy your completed query into the Query Tool and compare the

00:00:34.437 --> 00:00:41.357
output. Focus on the sequence: group first, calculate the count, then filter the groups using HAVING.

00:00:41.357 --> 00:00:45.392
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:50.467
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:53.997
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:58.347
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:58.347 --> 00:01:05.432
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:05.432 --> 00:01:11.807
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project: validate the meaning of the result,

00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:14.092
not only whether the statement executes.
